# Normalize line endings so the working tree matches CI.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true checks files out as CRLF on Windows while
# CI (Linux) sees LF. `biome check` then reports hundreds of phantom format
# errors locally that do not exist in CI — and, worse, hides the real ones in
# the noise. Storing LF and checking out LF everywhere keeps `pnpm lint`
# meaning the same thing on every machine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
*.onnx binary

# Lockfile churns hugely; keep it collapsed in review — but readable.
#
# This was `-diff`, which does not mean "collapse". It marks the file binary
# for diff purposes, so `git diff pnpm-lock.yaml` prints
#
#     Binary files a/pnpm-lock.yaml and b/pnpm-lock.yaml differ
#
# and `--stat` reports "Bin 323640 -> 322675 bytes, 0 insertions(+), 0
# deletions(-)". There is then no way to review a lockfile change locally at
# all — not `git diff`, not `git log -p`, not `git add -p`. On a lockfile that
# is the one review you actually want, because the question is always "did
# this bump a patch, or swap a package for one nobody chose?"
#
# It also was not doing the job it was added for: GitHub already collapses
# pnpm-lock.yaml in the PR view by its own generated-file detection, and
# `-diff` has no effect there. So the attribute bought nothing and cost the
# local diff.
#
# `linguist-generated=true` is the attribute that actually states the intent:
# collapsed by default on GitHub, excluded from language statistics, and fully
# diffable everywhere else.
pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated=true
